# Plan Files

## Location & Naming

Locations & path rules: see `core.mdc` ("Plans Location").

### Other Rules
- Multi-agent work: **ONE plan file** (`{feature}-agents.plan.md`), not separate files per agent

---

## Multi-Agent Plan Structure

All agents for a feature belong in a single plan file. This keeps coordination, boundaries, and handoffs visible in one place.

```markdown
# {Feature} Multi-Agent Plan

## Summary
What this plan does, in concrete terms (see [Plan Summary](#plan-summary)).

## Agent Overview
| Agent | Responsibility | Owns | Dependencies |
|-------|---------------|------|--------------|
| Agent 1 | Backend/service logic | {module}/services/… | None |
| Agent 2 | UI components | {ui}/components/… | None |
| Agent 3 | Wiring + shared state | {shared}/…, {state}/… | Agent 1 |

## Shared Files
| File | Owner | Rule |
|------|-------|------|
| {shared interface file} | Agent 3 | Others append their entries |
| {shared registry file} | Agent 3 | Others request additions |

---

## Agent 1: {Name}

### Prompt
> {Copy-paste ready prompt — see template below}

### Boundary
- **OWNS (exclusive write):** `{module}/services/…`
- **READS (no modify):** `{shared types file}`, `{shared registry file}`

### Tasks
- [ ] Task 1
- [ ] Task 2

### Deliverables
What this agent produces for others to consume.

---

## Agent 2: {Name}
... (same structure for each agent)
```

---

## Plan Summary

Every plan opens with a **Summary**, before the agent breakdown. Its job is
to **conceptualize the work in concrete terms** — what actually exists or
behaves differently once the plan is done — so a reader understands the
change itself, not just the tasks that produce it.

Write it for the change at hand:

- **New app or feature** — what it is, what it does, and who uses it.
- **Change to existing behavior** — how the system behaves today and how it
  behaves afterwards.
- **Refactor or infrastructure work** — what ends up structurally different
  and what stays the same for users.

A few sentences is enough. Don't restate the plan's title, and don't list
the tasks or agents — those follow below it in the plan.

### Keep it current

The Summary describes the plan as it stands, so it goes stale the moment
the plan changes. The **organizer** — single writer of the plan (see
`roles/organizer.mdc`) — updates the Summary in the **same edit** that
changes the plan, including when a
[deviation](#deviations-from-the-plan) is folded back in. A Summary that
no longer matches the rest of the plan is a defect in the plan.

---

## Agent Boundaries

Clear boundaries prevent agents from stepping on each other's work.

### Ownership Rules

1. **Exclusive ownership** — Each agent OWNS specific files/directories and has sole write access
2. **Read-only access** — Agents can read other agents' files but must not modify them
3. **Shared files** — Designate one owner; others either append to designated sections or request the owner to make changes

### Handling Conflicts

If an agent MUST touch another agent's file:

- Document why in the plan before starting
- Coordinate timing — wait until the owner completes their work
- Limit changes to specific lines or functions, not broad edits

### Interface Contracts (required for parallel execution)

Any multi-agent plan intended for **parallel execution** must define the
**interface contracts between modules** — not just file ownership. For
each boundary between agents, specify the concrete:

- **API surface** each agent exposes for others to call
- **Function/method signatures** at that boundary
- **Shared types / data shapes** exchanged between agents

Agents code against these agreed signatures instead of waiting to see a
peer's implementation, which is what lets them work in parallel without
stepping on each other. See `development/parallel-subagents.mdc` for execution.

### Deviations from the Plan

Legitimate deviations from an agreed plan are **allowed** — but they must
be **explicit and justified**, never silent divergence or scope creep.

- Every deviation is floated up to the final work summary for the
  **user** (see `human-interface/session-wrap-up-summary.mdc`). Resolution by the
  organizer does **not** exempt it: the user is told about every plan
  change, even ones the organizer already detected and handled mid-run.
- Deviations that affect a **subsystem or interface boundary** (a contract
  between agents) are escalated, not patched around peer-to-peer: the doer
  escalates and the organizer — single writer of the plan — re-plans and
  re-runs the affected agents. See `roles/doer.mdc` and `roles/organizer.mdc`.
- Mid-run interface-contract changes are rare and expensive. Once
  identified, re-plan / re-run rather than patching around them.

---

## Code Quality Expectations

All agent prompts should emphasize **high code quality and organization**:

- Clean, readable, well-documented code
- Proper error handling and edge cases
- Consistent naming conventions and file organization
- Follow existing patterns in the codebase

Language- and style-specific rules (naming, typing, imports, formatting)
live in `development/style.mdc`; "no shortcuts / tech debt" lives in
`development/no-hacky-features.mdc` — point agents at those rather than
restating them here.

Include this expectation in every agent prompt.

---

## Agent Prompt Template

Each agent's prompt must be **copy-paste ready** with clear scope, boundaries, and deliverables:

Concrete paths and commands come from the project's own structure and its
`AGENTS.md`; the placeholders below stand in for them.

```markdown
### Prompt
> Implement {feature} with high code quality.
>
> **Scope:**
> - Create the service/logic in `{owned module path}/`
> - Register any shared entries in `{shared registry file}` and wire handlers
> - Add types in `{shared types file}` (or the domain's types location)
> - Create UI components in `{owned ui path}/`
> - Add shared state in `{state path}/` if needed
> - Follow existing patterns and conventions
>
> **Boundaries:**
> - OWNS: `{owned module path}/…`, `{owned ui path}/…`
> - READS: `{shared types file}`, `{shared registry file}`
> - Do NOT modify: other agents' owned directories
>
> **Quality Requirements:**
> - High code quality and organization (see `development/style.mdc`)
> - No shortcuts or tech debt (see `development/no-hacky-features.mdc`)
> - Error handling and edge cases covered
> - Clean, readable, documented code
>
> **Deliverables:**
> - Working feature integrated with the rest of the system
> - A concise inline summary of what changed, for independent review (see `development/independent-review.mdc`)
>
> **When complete:** Run the project's lint/typecheck/build commands (see the project's `AGENTS.md`) and report results.
```

---

## Completion Summary

The **doer** ends with a concise **inline** summary of what it changed —
this is the input an independent reviewer audits, not a trusted record.
Keep it in the work summary (chat / PR), not a persisted file (doer
behavior: see `roles/doer.mdc`).

- **What changed** — files touched and why
- **Verification** — the tests, lint, typecheck, and build the agent ran
- **Deviations from plan** — any divergence and its justification (see
  [Deviations from the Plan](#deviations-from-the-plan))
- **Handoff notes** — what the next agent or the coordinator needs to know

Completed non-trivial work is then verified by a fresh reviewer instance
per `development/independent-review.mdc`.

---

## Plan Approval

Writing the plan and implementing it are **separate steps, separated by the
developer**. Planning ends at the **Plan ready?** gate — the developer's
call, not the organizer's (see `process.mdc`).

When the plan file is written or rewritten, **stop**:

- Tell the developer where the plan lives and give a short overview of what
  it will do (the plan's [Summary](#plan-summary) is that overview).
- **Wait for an explicit go-ahead** before implementing any of it. Silence,
  a clarifying answer, or a "looks good" on some unrelated point is not
  approval.
- Do **not** write the plan and start executing it in the same turn.

Being **asked for a plan is not approval to build it.** Only an explicit
instruction to implement, build, or execute opens the Development phase. If
it's genuinely ambiguous whether the developer is approving or still
discussing, **ask** — the cost of one question is far below that of an
unwanted implementation run.

Developer feedback at this gate loops back into the plan: the organizer —
single writer of the plan — revises it, updates the Summary to match, and
**returns to this same gate**. Each rewrite needs its own go-ahead.

---

## Execution

Once the developer has approved the plan (see
[Plan Approval](#plan-approval)) and asks for it to be implemented, the
**organizer** executes it per `development/parallel-subagents.mdc` and
`roles/organizer.mdc`:

- Confirm parallel (or sequential) subagent strategy **before** launching subagents
- Match the plan's Agent Overview, phases, and dependency footnotes

---

## Cleanup

When a plan is fully complete and verified:

- Prepend `COMPLETE-` to the filename, or
- Move to `.cursor/plans/completed/` subfolder
